data: [{"id":"e8af727fb7f09bbdd2c16de1d6ae35066b9e1ce49a69cc1ccd1945fe7ce9e365","type":"text","score":1,"text":"### 2.1 · The 2026 timeline that put the meter in your customer's tenant\n\nMay 1, 2026\n\n#### Agent 365 goes generally available\n\nMicrosoft's control plane for agents: inventory, identity and lifecycle. A per-user seat, not a meter. Business Premium qualifies.\n\nJune 1, 2026\n\n#### GitHub Copilot moves to AI credits\n\nEach license includes a pooled monthly allowance. Additional usage is on by default. Developers have been on a meter since.\n\nJuly 1, 2026\n\n#### Cowork usage billing goes live\n\nCopilot Cowork, the delegate-a-job product, starts drawing Copilot Credits. Nadella told investors in July that thousands of customers were already paying for it. The [Cowork Guide](cowork.html) covers the product; the [Cowork simulator](cowork-calculator.html) models the credits.\n\nJuly 29, 2026\n\n#### “Seat plus usage” becomes the investor language\n\nThe FY26 Q4 call: usage-based credit consumption in customer-service agents up four times quarter over quarter. Autopilots, autonomous long-running agents, announced.\n\nSeptember 1, 2026\n\n#### Microsoft starts paying partners for consumption\n\nThe Copilot Cowork CSP Activation Incentive recognizes partners “accelerating consumption growth.” The payout is gated on sustained monthly consumption, not on seats. See [section 11](#section-11).\n\nSeptember 1, 2026\n\n#### Building agents starts costing credits\n\nCopilot Studio developer and trial environments using the GitHub harness move to usage billing, from the first build action.\n\nSeptember 25, 2026\n\n#### The new Copilot, with FinOps for AI\n\nCowork, Code, Autopilot and frontier models all run on usage-based billing. Cost Management, spending policies, per-user limits, alerts and model routing live under Copilot in the Microsoft 365 admin center. Partner Center names FinOps for AI as the partner opportunity.\n\nOctober 1, 2026\n\n#### The default-on announcement, and growth margin\n\nPartner Center confirms usage-based billing on by default for new Copilot Business in CSP from December 1, with the 4,000-credit default. The same day, CSP growth margin goes live on Copilot seat growth.\n\nNovember 1, 2026\n\n#### AI Builder credits stop being sold\n\nNo new or renewed AI Builder add-ons, and new premium licenses no longer include them. When a customer's AI Builder credits run out, Power Automate's AI steps draw Copilot Credits, or stop if none are available.\n\nNovember 2, 2026\n\n#### Sandbox opens\n\nPartners can test the default-on experience before it reaches customers.\n\nDecember 1, 2026\n\n#### The meter is on for every new Copilot Business license in CSP\n\nPay-as-you-go by default. 4,000 credits per user per month by default. Billed through the partner's Azure plan. Eleven markets are excluded at launch, including Germany, France, India and Australia. The United States is in.\n\n[↑ Contents](#toc)\n\n## 3 · The Customer's New Problem\n\nYour customer did not ask for a meter. They bought Copilot the way they buy everything from Microsoft: a price per person, per month, that finance can put in a budget. What arrives on December 1 is that price plus a ceiling they did not set, on a bill they will not see until it lands. The chart below is the first thing to draw for a business owner.\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-token-economics.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"Token economics for the SMB Copilot practice. Copilot Credits for people, agents and business apps, and GitHub credits for developers: every Microsoft AI meter in your customer's tenant. The customer's new problem, the three sentences, the four-verb managed service on every meter, what to charge for each, the practice conversation, the talk track and three dated campaigns.","page_type":"playbook","priority":3,"title":"The Meter Conversation: Token Economics","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.59747005,"keyword_score":11.812887488363938,"vector_rank":10,"keyword_rank":4,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"927b4b8bd7b3551c79c18b306f8f10babc33a3d3a1250016a52b248a01716fb9","type":"text","score":0.9428596934979914,"text":"An Azure subscription linked to a Power Platform billing policy.\n\nIt lets use above any prepaid capacity bill as pay-as-you-go. Without some billing path, agent use that no license covers simply fails.\n\n**Cost:** $0.01 a credit.\n\nMicrosoft [Copilot Credits Guide, October 2026](https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2368800), October 8, 2026, p.4 · F-149\n\nNew CSP Copilot Business seats from Dec 1i\n\nNot sureYesNo\n\nWhether the Copilot Business seats are new CSP purchases on or after December 1, 2026.\n\nThose seats come with usage-based billing on and a default monthly limit per user.\n\n**Cost:** 4,000 credits per user a month by default.\n\nMicrosoft [Partner Center announcements, October 2026](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/announcements/2026-october), October 8, 2026 · F-157\n\nCountryi\n\nUnited StatesCanadaUnited KingdomIrelandAustralia (December 1 default not available at first)New ZealandSouth AfricaSingaporeJapanSouth Korea (December 1 default not available at first)India (December 1 default not available at first)Germany (December 1 default not available at first)France (December 1 default not available at first)Italy (December 1 default not available at first)Spain (December 1 default not available at first)Netherlands (December 1 default not available at first)Belgium (December 1 default not available at first)Poland (December 1 default not available at first)SwedenNorwayDenmarkFinlandSwitzerlandAustriaPortugalBrazil (December 1 default not available at first)MexicoUnited Arab EmiratesIsrael\n\nWhere the tenant is billed.\n\nIn 11 countries the December 1 default limit is not available at first; the list marks them.\n\nMicrosoft [Partner Center announcements, October 2026](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/announcements/2026-october), October 8, 2026 · F-156\n\nPlan timing\n\nPlan start monthi\n\nThe first of the twelve months this plan models.\n\nAgent go-live months count from here. The Copilot Business promotion applies while the plan starts by December 2026.\n\nMicrosoft [Partner Center announcements, July 2026](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/announcements/2026-july), July 29, 2026 · F-147\n\nWorking days a monthi\n\nWorking days in a typical month.\n\nUsed only to time when an average Cowork user reaches a per-user limit. Microsoft’s estimator assumes 22; most partners leave it.\n\nThe twelve months modeledi\n\nNovember 2026 to October 2027\n\nThe twelve months this plan covers, from the start month.\n\nThe pool, the purchase and the day-by-day enforcement all use these months.\n\nWhat this account looks like\n\n0\n\nCopilot-licensed users\n\nBusiness, enterprise and E7 seats\n\n50\n\nUsers without a Copilot license\n\nTheir agent use is metered\n\nDoes not apply\n\nDecember 1 default limit\n\nNo Copilot Business seats entered.\n\nNot sure\n\nAgent 365\n\nConfirm the tenant base or the Defender and Purview suites.\n\n2\n\n## The agents\n\nLoad a whole scenario in one click, or add agents one at a time. Every count is an exact field; every line shows its rate, its source and whether a Copilot license covers it.\n\nStart from a scenario (replaces the agents below; keeps the account)\n\nThree SMB agents\n\nWebsite FAQ, HR and IT helpdesk, and invoice intake: 40,600 credits a month.\n\nBusiness Central agents\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-credit-planner.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"TD SYNNEX partner planner for Copilot Credits: Cowork and Copilot Studio agents in one pool, the cheapest way to buy, the guardrails to set, and the conversation to have. Built on Microsoft's published rates.","page_type":"tool","priority":2,"title":"Copilot Credit Planner","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.64746034,"keyword_score":11.859289649690814,"vector_rank":1,"keyword_rank":2,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"bbb8ca5ede0bad712d6e4a9daa3796847bd84633eda0e6a7d6bcc355ea1f7e9c","type":"text","score":0.9392501654154302,"text":"| Stage  | SKU                        | Price                        | Key Limitation / Upgrade Signal                                                                                                                                       | Partner Revenue Range                                 |\n| ------ | -------------------------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| 1      | Copilot Chat               | Free                         | No M365 app integration; web-only                                                                                                                                     | Conversation starter only                             |\n| 2      | **M365 Copilot Business**  | $21/user/mo                  | Max 300 seats; requires Business Basic/Standard/Premium base                                                                                                          | Margin + $5K–$10K Data Readiness                      |\n| 2.5    | Personal Agent Creation    | Included in Copilot Business | Requires user enablement to drive adoption                                                                                                                            | $1.5K–$3K Enablement Workshops                        |\n| 3      | M365 Business Premium      | $22/user/mo                  | Max 300 seats; basic DLP/manual sensitivity labels only                                                                                                               | Margin + $10K–$15K Security Deployment                |\n| 3.5    | **Copilot Cowork** Agentic | Included in M365 Copilot     | Frontier program preview; requires flawless data governance                                                                                                           | Accelerates Premium/Purview + AgentOps pipeline       |\n| 4      | Copilot Studio             | $200/mo (25K msgs)           | Overage requires add-on message packs                                                                                                                                 | $15K–$50K+ Custom Agent Development                   |\n| 5–6    | E3 + M365 Copilot / E5     | $66 / $57/user/mo            | 300-seat cap hit or compliance outgrown manual labeling                                                                                                               | Licensing uplift + $50K+ Enterprise Migration         |\n| 7      | M365 E7 Frontier           | $99/user/mo (GA May 1, 2026) | Frontier destination; Agent 365 included                                                                                                                              | Managed AgentOps retainer opportunity                 |\n| Add-on | **Agent 365** New          | $15/user/mo (or in E7)       | Governance control plane for AI agents; not in E3/E5; enters at Stage 2 — see [The Agent Maturity Model](#9-the-agent-maturity-model-a-staircase-to-frontier-partner) | Managed governance retainer; pairs with AgentOps tier |\n\nKey Facts Every Partner Must Know\n\n**Copilot Business vs. Enterprise:** Copilot Business ($21/user/mo) launched December 2025 exclusively for SMBs (≤300 seats) on Business Basic/Standard/Premium. The Enterprise SKU ($30/user/mo) is for E3/E5 customers. AI functionality is identical — the difference is governance depth tied to the underlying base license.\n\n**Copilot Cowork (March 2026):** The shift from conversational AI to agentic AI. Users can delegate multi-step, long-running tasks — scheduling, research, document generation — that run autonomously in the background. Built into the Copilot license. Currently in Frontier program preview and represents the most significant new adoption driver in the SMB market.\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/cpb-model-the-practice.html","timestamp":1787875200000,"metadata":{"description":"The business model behind the practice: the Show-and-Tell sales motion, the Agent Maturity ladder, and the pricing and revenue architecture from first deployment to Managed AgentOps.","page_type":"playbook","priority":3,"title":"Model the Practice","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.62375444,"keyword_score":7.946141539113724,"vector_rank":3,"keyword_rank":26,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"e8af727fb7f09bbdd2c16de1d6ae3506d9ae30ec5957462f2357256599c95480","type":"text","score":0.9339171902838024,"text":"[↑ Contents](#toc)\n\n## 15 · Three Dates, Three Campaigns\n\nThree campaigns, each with a date or a reason that makes the first call easy.\n\nNovember 1 · The AI Builder sunset\n\nTo every customer whose flows use AI Builder: **“After November 1, Microsoft stops selling the AI credits your flows run on. Let's plan the move to Copilot Credits before a flow stops.”**\n\nDecember 1 · The people meter\n\n**The campaign below.** Every Copilot Business customer, before the default runs.\n\nAny week · The agent and developer sweep\n\nTo every customer with an agent, Business Central agents or GitHub seats: **“Your AI has meters with no limits. Thirty minutes to set them.”** The free session is SET; the close is AgentCare and developer governance.\n\nOther partners are offering the setup session for free. Turn it into the on-ramp to the paid service. This is the first thing to ship, and it is sellable to every Copilot Business customer you have, this quarter.\n\nThis week\n\n#### Email every Copilot Business customer\n\nSubject line: “Your Copilot bill changes December 1\\. Let's set your limit first.” Three sentences from [section 6](#section-6) and a link to book thirty minutes.\n\nNovember 2\n\n#### Run the default-on sandbox yourself first\n\nBecome Customer Zero on the meter. Set your own limits, trip your own alert, pull your own export. The [Customer Zero Starter Kit](customer-zero-starter-kit/index.html) is the method.\n\nNovember\n\n#### The thirty-minute SET session, free\n\nLink the Azure billing, lower the default, set the alerts, review auto-apply, name the budget owner. The customer leaves with a signed one-page credit budget.\n\nEnd of the session\n\n#### The close\n\n“That's SET. WATCH, TUNE and PROVE are $4 a person a month. Your first page arrives in thirty days.”\n\nDecember 1\n\n#### The meter is on, and your customers chose their number\n\nEveryone else's customers got the default.\n\n### 15.1 · Before you sell this\n\n* **Your own tenant has a spending policy, a per-user limit and a tripped alert in its history.** You cannot explain the 80% email until you have received one.\n* **You have pulled a consumption export** by user, group, agent and service, and turned it into one page, once.\n* **You have run the [Copilot Credit Planner](copilot-credit-planner.html) for one real customer** and can say which funding source it recommended and why.\n* **You can say the three sentences without notes**, and your seller can draw the four bars.\n* **Your MSA has a line for credit pass-through and an owner clause for commitments.**\n* **You have set a per-agent limit and seen what happens when an agent hits it**, in your own tenant.\n* **You have set a GitHub budget on your own organization.**\n\n[↑ Contents](#toc)\n\n## 16 · Guardrails: What Never To Say\n\nTrap 1\n\n#### “A credit is about N tokens.”\n\nMicrosoft publishes no conversion, and per-task consumption varies by model, context and tools. An automated summary of the Credits Guide invented one; the actual document has none. The credit is the unit. Stop there.\n\nTrap 2\n\n#### “The default limit is $10 a user.”\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-token-economics.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"Token economics for the SMB Copilot practice. Copilot Credits for people, agents and business apps, and GitHub credits for developers: every Microsoft AI meter in your customer's tenant. The customer's new problem, the three sentences, the four-verb managed service on every meter, what to charge for each, the practice conversation, the talk track and three dated campaigns.","page_type":"playbook","priority":3,"title":"The Meter Conversation: Token Economics","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.5715495,"keyword_score":11.78068726182981,"vector_rank":24,"keyword_rank":5,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"e8af727fb7f09bbdd2c16de1d6ae3506f17e230eff928575c470e8e2155eff7c","type":"text","score":0.916475864560971,"text":"## 1 · The Answer, On One Page\n\nThis playbook started with a question partners could not answer. They could explain the $30 to $40 a user they charge on top of a Microsoft 365 license: helpdesk, security, backup, patching. Asked what they did for the Copilot license, they went quiet. The three tiers in [Model the Practice](cpb-model-the-practice.html) answered that. Now the question has changed shape, and Microsoft changed it on purpose.\n\nThe seat was the key. _The credit is the meter._ Microsoft built the meters, switched most of them on by default, set the defaults above the license, and routed the biggest bill through you. Nobody is reading them.\n\nThe people meter switches on December 1\\. The others are already running. The partner who can say three plain sentences about the meter, and sell a four-verb service behind them, owns the conversation that now sits inside every Copilot license sale.\n\n$40\n\nWhat the default lets each Copilot Business user spend on top of the $21 seat: 4,000 credits at $0.01\n\nPartner Center, October 1, 2026\\. On from December 1.\n\nNone\n\nThe default credit limit Microsoft sets on an agent. A website agent bills every answer until someone sets one\n\nMicrosoft Learn, govern credit consumption, October 2026\n\nOn\n\nGitHub Copilot paid usage, by default, for every organization, with no budget set\n\nGitHub documentation, since June 1, 2026\n\nTHE PROBLEM\n\n#### Your customer bought flat numbers and got meters\n\nEveryday Copilot is still in the seat. The work Copilot does, the agents that answer customers, the business-app agents that process invoices, and the AI developers use all run on meters. A credit is a penny on each of them, and the customer chose none of the defaults.\n\nTHE SERVICE\n\n#### Four verbs, every meter, once a month\n\n**SET** the budget and the limits. **WATCH** the meter. **TUNE** who buys what. **PROVE** it on one page. Only the admin screen changes from meter to meter.\n\nTHE MONEY\n\n#### Price each meter in the unit that drives it\n\nPer user for people, per agent for agents, per developer for developers. Credits pass through at Microsoft's price. Your fee is the predictable line, with a floor under each one.\n\n[↑ Contents](#toc)\n\n## 2 · What Changed: Seats Plus Usage\n\nFor twenty years the Microsoft business you sold was a seat. One price, one user, one month. In 2026 Microsoft said, in its own words, that the model is now the seat plus what the user consumes. The words below are Microsoft's, not ours. They are the corroboration a partner needs before building a practice on this.\n\nIn Microsoft's words, 2026\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-token-economics.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"Token economics for the SMB Copilot practice. Copilot Credits for people, agents and business apps, and GitHub credits for developers: every Microsoft AI meter in your customer's tenant. The customer's new problem, the three sentences, the four-verb managed service on every meter, what to charge for each, the practice conversation, the talk track and three dated campaigns.","page_type":"playbook","priority":3,"title":"The Meter Conversation: Token Economics","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.5556326,"keyword_score":12.65730296931278,"vector_rank":38,"keyword_rank":1,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"e8af727fb7f09bbdd2c16de1d6ae3506a05b710f5d97d950b8eb41dce3f9e61c","type":"text","score":0.9060080890570033,"text":"## 8 · The Whiteboard\n\nEvery MSP owner can draw the first bar from memory. The second bar is what this playbook taught. The third and fourth bars are what December 1 adds, and the difference between them is the service.\n\nChart C · Old math, Copilot math, and the new fourth line\n\nPer user, per month. The top two bars you already sell. The third is Microsoft's, and it is on whether you like it or not. The fourth is what stops the third from surprising anyone.\n\nFour stacked bars comparing per-user monthly economics The Microsoft 365 math: $22 license plus $35 managed services, $57\\. The Copilot math: $21 license plus $12 Tier 1, $33\\. December 1 left on the default: $21 plus $12 plus a $40 credit ceiling, $73\\. December 1 governed: $21 plus $12 plus a $15 credit budget plus $4 Credit Wrap, $52. The Microsoft 365 math you already sell Microsoft 365 Business Premium license: $22 $22 Your managed services upcharge: $30 to $40, drawn at $35 $30 to $40 $57 The Copilot math the playbook taught Copilot Business license: $21 $21 Tier 1 AI Essentials: $12 (range $10 to $15) $12 $33 December 1, left on the default Copilot Business license: $21 $21 Tier 1 AI Essentials: $12 $12 Default credit ceiling: up to $40, nobody watching up to $40 in credits $73 December 1, governed Copilot Business license: $21 $21 Tier 1 AI Essentials: $12 $12 Credit budget the customer chose: $15 cap, passed through at Microsoft's price $15 cap Credit Wrap: $4 a user to read the meter $4 $52 $0 per user per month $80 \n\nMicrosoft licenseYour serviceCopilot Credits, passed throughCredit Wrap\n\n| Line                 | Microsoft license | Your service | Credits (pass-through) | Credit Wrap | Per user per month |\n| -------------------- | ----------------- | ------------ | ---------------------- | ----------- | ------------------ |\n| Microsoft 365 math   | $22               | $35          | —                      | —           | $57                |\n| Copilot math         | $21               | $12          | —                      | —           | $33                |\n| December 1, default  | $21               | $12          | up to $40              | —           | up to $73          |\n| December 1, governed | $21               | $12          | $15 cap                | $4          | $52                |\n\nThe $35 managed-services line is the midpoint of the $30 to $40 upcharge partners describe for Business Premium. The $15 credit cap and $4 Credit Wrap are the worked example in [section 9](#section-9), not Microsoft figures.\n\nThe whiteboard sentence\n\n“The top two lines you already know. The third line is Microsoft's, and it is on whether you like it or not. The fourth line is what stops the third one from surprising you.”\n\nThe second whiteboard, for agents\n\nOne line per agent: the build, once. The care, every month, priced by who the agent talks to. The credits, passed through. “Agents pay you twice: once to build, every month to run.”\n\n[↑ Contents](#toc)\n\n## 9 · Pricing Every Meter\n\n### 9.1 · Where the playbook is today, and why it moves\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-token-economics.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"Token economics for the SMB Copilot practice. Copilot Credits for people, agents and business apps, and GitHub credits for developers: every Microsoft AI meter in your customer's tenant. The customer's new problem, the three sentences, the four-verb managed service on every meter, what to charge for each, the practice conversation, the talk track and three dated campaigns.","page_type":"playbook","priority":3,"title":"The Meter Conversation: Token Economics","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.5557697,"keyword_score":11.853075913573234,"vector_rank":37,"keyword_rank":3,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"e8af727fb7f09bbdd2c16de1d6ae3506b1cc21bd401d48d3b88bba23b9181f94","type":"text","score":0.8933205633347479,"text":"### 4.3 · The business-app meter\n\nYour Business Central and Power Automate customers are on the meter without a Copilot seat\n\nBusiness Central's Payables agent is 50 credits an invoice plus 5 a line, and the Expense agent is 50 a receipt. Neither is covered by the Copilot included in Business Central. They draw from the default Power Platform environment unless you relink them. Separately, AI Builder credits can no longer be bought or renewed after November 1, 2026, and new premium licenses stop including them. When a customer's AI Builder credits run out, Power Automate's AI steps draw Copilot Credits, or stop if none are available. Every Business Central and Power Automate customer is heading onto the meter whether or not they bought Copilot.\n\n### 4.4 · The developer meter\n\n* **Since June 1, 2026, GitHub bills Copilot usage in AI credits.** A credit is a penny. Copilot Business includes 1,900 a license a month and costs $19; Enterprise includes 3,900 and costs $39. The allowance pools across the organization.\n* **Paid usage is on by default.** GitHub's words: “Additional usage is enabled by default.” No budget is set for you.\n* **When a budget runs out, work stops.** “There is no automatic fallback to lower-cost models.” A developer hits a wall mid-release.\n* **The controls exist:** user budgets, cost-center budgets, enterprise spending limits, alerts at 75, 90 and 100%, a switch that stops paid usage, and in-product requests for more.\n* **It is not on your invoice.** GitHub's Azure billing does not support the CSP Azure plan, so the customer pays GitHub directly. That is why nobody watches it, and why the service is governance, not resale.\n* **Two GitHub meters, not one.** The GitHub harness inside Copilot Studio bills Copilot Credits on your invoice. GitHub Copilot for developers bills GitHub credits on theirs.\n\n### 4.5 · The watch list\n\n| Meter                | What it is                                                                | Why it is not on the price sheet yet                                                                    |\n| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Security Copilot** | Security compute units, billed by the provisioned hour, with overage      | No Business Premium inclusion. It comes with E5 and E7, not with the licenses most SMB customers carry. |\n| **Azure AI Foundry** | Per-token model prices plus agent compute and tools, as an Azure resource | Opt-in Azure consumption for custom builds. It exists only where someone built something.               |\n| **Fabric Copilot**   | Copilot inside Microsoft Fabric, drawing Fabric capacity units            | Rare at 30 to 300 seats. Few SMB tenants run Fabric capacity.                                           |\n| **Agent 365**        | Microsoft's control plane for agents: inventory, identity and lifecycle   | A $15 seat, not a meter. Sell it as a seat when a customer needs the control plane.                     |\n\nSame four verbs if a customer has one. Price it when it appears in more than a handful of your tenants.\n\n### 4.6 · Which of your customers has which meter\n\nThe people meter\n\n**Every Copilot Business customer** has the people meter from December 1\\. That is your whole Copilot book.\n\nThe agent meter\n\n**Every customer with an agent or Business Central** has the agent meter. Pull the agent inventory and the Business Central list.\n\nThe developer meter\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-token-economics.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"Token economics for the SMB Copilot practice. Copilot Credits for people, agents and business apps, and GitHub credits for developers: every Microsoft AI meter in your customer's tenant. The customer's new problem, the three sentences, the four-verb managed service on every meter, what to charge for each, the practice conversation, the talk track and three dated campaigns.","page_type":"playbook","priority":3,"title":"The Meter Conversation: Token Economics","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.5792487,"keyword_score":8.36607010507547,"vector_rank":15,"keyword_rank":21,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"e8af727fb7f09bbdd2c16de1d6ae350674e08e43ab67a817bb3ec268f081d946","type":"text","score":0.8811343978500107,"text":"## 13 · The Talk Track\n\nWritten for a seller or vCIO talking to an SMB owner or office manager, 30 to 300 seats, who already buys Microsoft 365 and either has Copilot or is about to. Plain words. No “FinOps,” no “tokens,” no “UBB” in the customer's hearing.\n\n### 13.1 · Opening, sixty seconds\n\nYou say\n\n“You know how your Microsoft 365 bill has always been a flat number per person? Copilot changed that this year. The license is still flat, $21 a person. But the new stuff, the part where Copilot actually does work for you instead of answering questions, runs on a meter. Microsoft calls the units credits. A credit is a penny.\n\nHere's the thing nobody is telling small businesses. Starting December 1, every new Copilot Business license comes with that meter switched on, and the default setting lets each person spend up to 4,000 credits a month. That's forty dollars. On top of a twenty-one dollar license. For your 50 people, that is a $2,000 a month ceiling you did not agree to.\n\nThe bill comes to us first, because we are your Microsoft partner. So we are not going to let the default run. We are going to set it with you.”\n\n### 13.2 · The whiteboard moment\n\nDraw the four bars from [section 8](#section-8). Say the three sentences from [section 6](#section-6). Stop talking. Let them ask what a credit buys.\n\n### 13.3 · “What does a credit actually buy?”\n\nYou say\n\n“Three kinds of work, three price tags.\n\n**Asking Copilot a question** in Word, Outlook, Teams: included in the license. No meter.\n\n**Telling Copilot to go do a job**, like ‘build me the board pack from these six folders’ or ‘work through these forty invoices’: that runs on the meter, and a job is counted in dollars, not pennies. Microsoft's own range for a heavy user is somewhere between eighty and two hundred-odd dollars a month if nobody is watching.\n\n**An agent you build that answers your customers, or runs by itself overnight:** every answer and every action is metered. A couple of pennies per answer, a nickel per action.\n\nOur job is to make sure the second and third kinds happen on purpose.”\n\n### 13.4 · The offer, ninety seconds\n\nYou say\n\n“Four things, every month, for $4 a person.\n\nWe **SET** the budget with you: a limit per person, a limit for the whole company, an alert at 80%. We **WATCH** the meter: who is using it, what is sitting idle, anything that spikes. We **TUNE** it: steady users go on the cheaper prepaid rate, unused seats and forgotten agents get turned off, routine work goes to the cheaper model. And we **PROVE** it: one page, once a month, that says what you spent and what you got for it.\n\nThe credits themselves are Microsoft's price, on their own line. We don't mark up the pennies. We charge for reading the meter.”\n\n### 13.5 · The ask\n\nYou say\n\n“Before December 1, let's set your limits. Thirty minutes, in your admin center, with you on the call. Then we run one month at the budget we agreed and I bring you the first page.”\n\n### 13.6 · When the customer has an agent\n\nYou say\n\n“Before we build an agent, we decide who it talks to. If it's your licensed staff in Teams, it's free to run. If it's your customers, every answer costs a couple of pennies, so we put a limit on it the day it goes live and an alert well before the limit. An agent that runs on its own is a meter that runs while you sleep. We read it every week.”\n\n### 13.7 · When the customer runs Business Central or Power Automate\n\nYou say\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-token-economics.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"Token economics for the SMB Copilot practice. Copilot Credits for people, agents and business apps, and GitHub credits for developers: every Microsoft AI meter in your customer's tenant. The customer's new problem, the three sentences, the four-verb managed service on every meter, what to charge for each, the practice conversation, the talk track and three dated campaigns.","page_type":"playbook","priority":3,"title":"The Meter Conversation: Token Economics","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.56027275,"keyword_score":9.810086810436644,"vector_rank":30,"keyword_rank":11,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"e8af727fb7f09bbdd2c16de1d6ae35065fb833c040c34ecd3620aae33464f640","type":"text","score":0.8590923700348944,"text":"| “Our agents only serve our staff.”                             | “Then they're free, if your staff are licensed and use them in Teams or Copilot. Unlicensed staff and anything that runs on its own bill. We'll check every one.”                                                                               | Zero-rating rule; Copilot Chat agent guidance                                    |\n| “Just set the hard stop and we're safe.”                       | “The stop turns the agent off. Your customers see a dead chat window. We set the alert well below it and decide together before it trips.”                                                                                                      | Power Platform admin center, per-agent limits                                    |\n| “$750 a month for one agent?”                                  | “Not for an internal one; that's a couple hundred. The $750 is for agents that talk to your customers, where the bill and the brand risk live. Market retainers for one or two automations start at $500.”                                      | Market evidence, [section 5](#section-5)                                         |\n| “Business Central's Copilot is included.”                      | “The everyday Copilot features are. The Payables, Expense and Sales Order agents run on credits.”                                                                                                                                               | Business Central consumption billing documentation                               |\n| “GitHub is the developers' problem.”                           | “It's the budget owner's problem the day the bill arrives or the tool stops mid-release. Paid usage is on by default and there's no budget until someone sets one.”                                                                             | GitHub billing documentation                                                     |\n| “Won't capping it hurt your Microsoft incentives?”             | “A cap you chose keeps the agent alive. An unwatched bill gets it switched off.”                                                                                                                                                                | [Section 11](#section-11); Gartner                                               |\n\n[↑ Contents](#toc)\n\n## 15 · Three Dates, Three Campaigns\n\nThree campaigns, each with a date or a reason that makes the first call easy.\n\nNovember 1 · The AI Builder sunset\n\nTo every customer whose flows use AI Builder: **“After November 1, Microsoft stops selling the AI credits your flows run on. Let's plan the move to Copilot Credits before a flow stops.”**\n\nDecember 1 · The people meter\n\n**The campaign below.** Every Copilot Business customer, before the default runs.\n\nAny week · The agent and developer sweep\n\nTo every customer with an agent, Business Central agents or GitHub seats: **“Your AI has meters with no limits. Thirty minutes to set them.”** The free session is SET; the close is AgentCare and developer governance.\n\nOther partners are offering the setup session for free. Turn it into the on-ramp to the paid service. This is the first thing to ship, and it is sellable to every Copilot Business customer you have, this quarter.\n\nThis week\n\n#### Email every Copilot Business customer\n\nSubject line: “Your Copilot bill changes December 1\\. Let's set your limit first.” Three sentences from [section 6](#section-6) and a link to book thirty minutes.\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-token-economics.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"Token economics for the SMB Copilot practice. Copilot Credits for people, agents and business apps, and GitHub credits for developers: every Microsoft AI meter in your customer's tenant. The customer's new problem, the three sentences, the four-verb managed service on every meter, what to charge for each, the practice conversation, the talk track and three dated campaigns.","page_type":"playbook","priority":3,"title":"The Meter Conversation: Token Economics","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.5489749,"keyword_score":10.743804948460605,"vector_rank":44,"keyword_rank":8,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"6134b353d0773b88ca40ea6c1031afdf47b4b8c52917296e2f68d68c4f432d7f","type":"text","score":0.8224216458043745,"text":"Narrowing the claim wins you the deal\n\nMost partner marketing overstates regulatory applicability to small business, and sophisticated buyers know it. **EU AI Act:** enforcement provisions apply from 2 August 2026, but high-risk obligations moved to December 2027 and August 2028, and an SMB using Copilot is a _deployer_, not a provider. **Colorado:** SB 24-205 was repealed and replaced; the replacement takes effect 1 January 2027\\. **California ADMT:** 1 January 2027, gated behind CCPA thresholds most sub-100-seat businesses do not meet. When a customer says “I read the EU AI Act is going to hit us,” the winning answer is “probably not, and here is why.”\n\n[↑ Contents](#toc)\n\n## 13 · Claims Discipline\n\n### 13.1 · Do not repeat these\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-readiness-governance.html","timestamp":1787875200000,"metadata":{"description":"The Copilot readiness and governance engagement, phase by phase — the decisions, gates, checklists, effort bands and prices a partner needs in the room. For SMB customers on Business Premium who are not buying E5.","page_type":"engagement","priority":3,"title":"The Copilot Readiness &amp; Governance Engagement","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.54938895,"keyword_score":8.826955459723266,"vector_rank":43,"keyword_rank":16,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"927b4b8bd7b3551c79c18b306f8f10ba797dee8510d1364218b10aa18c981e4a","type":"text","score":0.8128415882467999,"text":"## The account\n\nWho the agents serve and which Copilot licenses they already hold. Licensed people signed in with Microsoft, in Teams, SharePoint or Copilot Chat, cost nothing to run; everyone else is metered.\n\nWho and how many\n\nPrepared by (your company)i\n\nYour company name, printed as “Prepared by” on the customer report and the governance sheet.\n\nLeave it blank to print without a name.\n\nTotal usersi\n\nFrom the Cowork tab\n\nEveryone at the customer who could use an internal agent, Copilot-licensed or not.\n\nThe Cowork tab fills it when it reports; type over it to keep your own figure.\n\n**Cost:** Copilot Business is capped at 300 seats, so above that some users need the enterprise SKU.\n\nMicrosoft [Partner Center announcements, July 2026](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/announcements/2026-july), July 29, 2026 · F-147\n\nCopilot Business seatsi\n\nFrom the Cowork tab\n\nCapped at 300 seats.\n\nMicrosoft 365 Copilot Business licenses the customer holds or is buying.\n\nLicensed staff using an agent in Teams, SharePoint or Copilot Chat with their Microsoft sign-in cost nothing to run.\n\n**Cost:** $18 a user a month on the promotion through December 31, 2026; then $21 annual or $25.20 monthly.\n\nMicrosoft [Partner Center announcements, July 2026](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/announcements/2026-july), July 29, 2026 · F-147\n\nMicrosoft 365 Copilot seatsi\n\nFrom the Cowork tab\n\nThe enterprise SKU.\n\nMicrosoft 365 Copilot licenses on the enterprise SKU.\n\nCovered the same way as Business seats.\n\n**Cost:** $30 a user a month.\n\nMicrosoft [Microsoft 365 Copilot for enterprise](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/solutions/enterprise), October 11, 2026 · F-090\n\nE7 seatsi\n\nFrom the Cowork tab\n\nMicrosoft 365 E7 licenses. E7 includes Microsoft 365 Copilot.\n\nThese users count as Copilot-licensed when they use an agent.\n\nTenant and billing\n\nTenant basei\n\nBusiness BasicBusiness StandardBusiness PremiumMicrosoft 365 E3Microsoft 365 E5 or E7Not sure\n\nFrom the Cowork tab\n\nThe Microsoft 365 plan most of the customer’s users are on.\n\nAgent 365 needs Business Premium, E5 or E7 (or the Defender and Purview suites).\n\n**Cost:** Agent 365 is $15 a user a month.\n\nMicrosoft [Partner Center announcements, July 2026](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/announcements/2026-july), July 29, 2026 · F-009\n\nDefender and Purview suites ownedi\n\nNot sureYesNo\n\nWhether the customer owns both the Microsoft Defender Suite and the Microsoft Purview Suite.\n\nOwning both qualifies a tenant on another base for Agent 365.\n\nMicrosoft [Partner Center announcements, July 2026](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/announcements/2026-july), July 29, 2026 · F-009\n\nBusiness Central in usei\n\nNoYes\n\nWhether the customer runs Dynamics 365 Business Central.\n\nWhen yes, the Business Central agents are listed first when you add an agent. They bill per document and draw from the default environment unless relinked.\n\nMicrosoft [Business Central: manage consumption-based billing (updated 2026-07-08)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dynamics365/business-central/dev-itpro/administration/tenant-admin-center-manage-consumption-billing), February 4, 2026 · F-188\n\nCopilot Business billing after the promotioni\n\nAnnual commitmentMonthly billing\n\nHow Copilot Business renews once the promotion ends.\n\nSets the seat price the seat-or-meter test uses after December 2026.\n\n**Cost:** Annual $21 or monthly $25.20 a user a month.\n\nMicrosoft [Microsoft 365 Copilot Business](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/solutions/business), October 11, 2026 · F-176\n\nAzure subscription linked for pay-as-you-goi\n\nNot sureYesNo\n\nAn Azure subscription linked to a Power Platform billing policy.\n\nIt lets use above any prepaid capacity bill as pay-as-you-go. Without some billing path, agent use that no license covers simply fails.\n\n**Cost:** $0.01 a credit.\n\nMicrosoft [Copilot Credits Guide, October 2026](https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2368800), October 8, 2026, p.4 · F-149\n\nNew CSP Copilot Business seats from Dec 1i\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-credit-planner.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"TD SYNNEX partner planner for Copilot Credits: Cowork and Copilot Studio agents in one pool, the cheapest way to buy, the guardrails to set, and the conversation to have. Built on Microsoft's published rates.","page_type":"tool","priority":2,"title":"Copilot Credit Planner","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.63627535,"keyword_score":7.603253729371397,"vector_rank":2,"keyword_rank":29,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"927b4b8bd7b3551c79c18b306f8f10ba1892f73766444bc3d48685f9d8e2637a","type":"text","score":0.7677280394960104,"text":"* Copilot in 30: a 25-user, 30-day Microsoft 365 Copilot Business trial, partner-led through CSP, for customers with fewer than 300 employees, through December 31, 2026\\. Microsoft [Partner Center announcements, August 2026](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/announcements/2026-august), August 25, 2026 · F-195\n* CSP growth margin (from October 1, 2026, for direct-bill partners and distributors) covers Microsoft 365 Copilot, E5, E7, Defender Suite, Purview Suite and Windows 365 Enterprise. Copilot Studio is not on the list. Microsoft [Partner Center announcements, October 2026](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/announcements/2026-october), October 8, 2026 · F-196\n* The FY27 Copilot Agents Voucher excludes CSP customers. Rules out a CSP deal Microsoft [FY27 Copilot Agents Voucher Program (Featured Program Center KB-01841)](https://fpc.microsoft.com/knowledgebase/article/KB-01841/en-us), July 1, 2026 · F-197\n* The Copilot Cowork activation incentive exists; its terms are gated, so quote nothing from it until you have read them. [Partner Center announcements, October 2026](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/announcements/2026-october), October 8, 2026\n\nAgentic Business Solutions specialization, Pathway Two\n\nCopilot Studio consumption here reaches $0 in twelve months, $10,000 short of the $10,000 bar per deployment. The association type must be CPOR, CSP T1 or CSP T2\\. Microsoft [Partner Center announcements, August 2026](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/announcements/2026-august), August 25, 2026 · F-178 [The specialization guide](copilot-specialization.html#s4)\n\nCopilot Credit plan\n\nOctober 12, 2026 · Rate card 2026-10-11\n\nEstimate, not a quote. Figures reflect the inputs shown and Microsoft list prices current at the rate card date.\n\n—\n\nCowork, credits a month\n\nNot included in this plan\n\n—\n\nCopilot Studio agents, credits a month\n\n0 agents\n\n0\n\nAll in, a steady month\n\n$0 at Microsoft list\n\n0\n\nCredits a month your Copilot licenses already cover\n\nLicensed people, signed in, on Microsoft channels\n\n#### Per tool, a steady month Modeled\n\n| Tool     | Credits a month | A month at Microsoft list | Note                |\n| -------- | --------------- | ------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| The pool | 0               | $0                        | Year one: 0 credits |\n\n#### How to buy it\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-credit-planner.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"TD SYNNEX partner planner for Copilot Credits: Cowork and Copilot Studio agents in one pool, the cheapest way to buy, the guardrails to set, and the conversation to have. Built on Microsoft's published rates.","page_type":"tool","priority":2,"title":"Copilot Credit Planner","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.60668087,"keyword_score":7.4770732029497555,"vector_rank":8,"keyword_rank":32,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"afe4900fae1a89d6af9a744264380103f109e078a46f6d9b2026a21f01c4bba2","type":"text","score":0.7501242466039372,"text":"These are annual-term subscriptions: cancellation is limited to a short window at the start of the term, and seat counts cannot be reduced until renewal — plan the count as carefully as the price. The term mechanics are covered in the [Azure Billing Setup guide](azure-billing-setup.html).\n\nWhy this one\n\nThis path has to cover 80 Copilot users — under the 300-seat cap, so the SMB bundles are available to you.\n\nThis path closes 3 of the 3 gaps you flagged: Purview sensitivity labels, DLP for AI, Defender for Cloud Apps.\n\nWe are not recommending Business Premium with Copilot even though it is $15.00/user/month cheaper all-in — $1,200/month across 80 seats — because it leaves 2 of those gaps open on a tenant that is about to send its own data through an agent.\n\nStill open after any license change: Usage-based billing enabled — Microsoft lists this as a Cowork prerequisite. No SKU closes it — it is an admin action in Copilot > Cost Management.\n\nIf IT tells you those controls are already on, change your answers in the readiness step and this recommendation rewrites itself.\n\nOpen until someone confirms otherwise\n\n⚠\n\n**Usage-based billing enabled** — Microsoft lists this as a Cowork prerequisite. No SKU closes it — it is an admin action in Copilot > Cost Management. Microsoft\n\n✗\n\n**Purview sensitivity labels** — Not asked. Until someone confirms it, assume labels are not configured — unlabeled data is retrieved at the same credit cost with none of the controls. Estimate\n\n✗\n\n**DLP for AI** — Not asked. Until someone confirms it, assume nothing is inspecting what leaves via an agent. Estimate\n\n✗\n\n**Defender for Cloud Apps** — Not asked. Until someone confirms it, assume there is no visibility into unsanctioned AI tools alongside Cowork. Estimate\n\nReadiness & flags2 to review · governance not asked\n\n?\n\n**Governance readiness was not asked.** These five are your discovery list for the next call — not findings about this tenant. Everything below treats them as open, which can only over-state what they need, never under-state it.\n\nAsk them now — about thirty seconds →\n\n⚠\n\nNew Copilot Business licenses bought through CSP from December 1, 2026 switch usage-based billing on by default, with a default limit of 4,000 Copilot Credits per user per month (about $40). This account models about 14,519 credits per active Cowork user a month, so a user reaches that default around working day 7 of the month and their usage stops until an admin raises it. If these seats are bought on or after that date, raise the per-user limit in Copilot → Cost Management to about 30,000 credits (the top of the band for the heaviest persona, Technical, at about 22,800 a month) before go-live. Not yet in effect in Australia, Belgium, Brazil, France, Germany, India, Italy, Korea, the Netherlands, Poland or Spain.\n\nℹ\n\nOne answer decides every partner-economics figure on this page: do you hold the Azure billing account for this customer, or do you buy through an indirect provider? Set it at the top of the pricing screen. Until then the tool shows the customer’s numbers in full and withholds yours — a margin calculation shown to a partner who cannot earn it is worse than none.\n\nThe next step\n\nConfirm before you quote\n\nRung 1 — Shadow AI Assessment\n\n$1,500–$7,500 one-time\n\n\"Here are the AI tools your staff used last month, and here is the client data that left your environment.\" One-time, fixed fee — and it answers the billing and governance questions in the same pass.\n\nNobody has confirmed the Azure plan or usage-based billing yet, and both are hard prerequisites.\n\nThe assessment is the cheapest way to answer them and everything else at the same time.\n\n**And then:** Rung 2, the Acceptable Use Policy, sells immediately off the back of it. Never sell a policy without an enforcement plan attached.\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/cowork-calculator.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"Model what Microsoft Cowork actually costs a customer — licenses, credits, PayGo vs. P3, and the talk track — at Microsoft list price or your own sell rates. A free interactive calculator for Copilot partners.","page_type":"tool","priority":2,"title":"Cowork Cost &amp; Conversation Simulator","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.5613873,"keyword_score":8.91214963541939,"vector_rank":28,"keyword_rank":14,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"6134b353d0773b88ca40ea6c1031afdf5e9fdeac1114e19fbd91b31980958276","type":"text","score":0.7222941794428881,"text":"## 1 · The Answer, On One Page\n\nYour customer is on Business Premium and is not buying E5\\. Everything below assumes that as the starting condition.\n\nThe data-protection half\n\n**Fully reachable on Business Premium.** Labels, DLP, retention, insider risk, audit, and the control that keeps labeled content out of Copilot — all through the Purview Suite for Business Premium add-on at $10 per user per month.\n\nThe permissions half\n\n**One gap, and only one.** SharePoint Advanced Management requires an Office 365 or Microsoft 365 E-SKU base. Business Premium and Business Standard are not on Microsoft's list, and buying Copilot does not change that.\n\nThe three routes across it\n\n**None of them is E5.** Deliver natively on Business Premium at zero license cost; use a governance platform that does not depend on SharePoint Advanced Management; or move the base to an enterprise SKU. [Section 5](#section-5).\n\n$10\n\nPurview Suite for Business Premium, per user per month MS list\n\n300-seat cap · half price alongside Copilot through 31 Dec 2026\n\n3\n\nGates that must be true before enablement\n\nEverything else on the list can follow\n\n40–60\n\nRemediation hours at 50 seats over 30 days Range\n\nNot the 200–400 the channel repeats\n\n\\~7 wks\n\nScan to live, governed pilot\n\n85–145 partner hours end to end Model\n\n### 1.1 · The two ways partners lose this deal\n\nFailure one — sequence\n\n**Opening with the governance work.** The customer arrived with demand; you replaced it with an obligation before establishing that anything was wrong in their tenant. Establish the problem, then sell the fix.\n\nFailure two — conclusion\n\n**Finding the SharePoint Advanced Management requirement and quoting E5.** That conclusion is wrong. It is one of three routes and the one the customer is least likely to accept.\n\n### 1.2 · The seven phases at a glance\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-readiness-governance.html","timestamp":1787875200000,"metadata":{"description":"The Copilot readiness and governance engagement, phase by phase — the decisions, gates, checklists, effort bands and prices a partner needs in the room. For SMB customers on Business Premium who are not buying E5.","page_type":"engagement","priority":3,"title":"The Copilot Readiness &amp; Governance Engagement","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.5515446,"keyword_score":6.4816494286303055,"vector_rank":42,"keyword_rank":50,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"31983b61ef98b33a0270b0e1c8899f9621c35506816b3fb1011cf1d222c195c0","type":"text","score":0.6338031199569663,"text":"---\ndescription: The Copilot Studio agent engagement, phase by phase — what must be true in the tenant, the rate card, the caps, the drift register and the recurring service. Seven phases, priced and staffed, two of them before any paper. For SMB customers on Business Premium with zero Copilot seats.\ntitle: The Copilot Studio Agent Engagement\n---\n\n[ Copilot Playbook ](index.html) \n\n[Copilot Playbook](index.html)/Copilot Studio/The Copilot Studio Agent Engagement\n\nUpdated October 11, 2026\n\nEngagement Guide · Seven Phases, Priced and Staffed\n\n# The Copilot Studio Agent Engagement\n\nSeven phases from the first conversation to the retainer — what must be true in the tenant at each step, what the meter costs, which controls to set before launch, and what you are still being paid for two years later. For the customers you actually have: Business Premium, zero Copilot seats. The agent is never done, and that is the commercial argument.\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-studio-agent-engagement.html","timestamp":1791676800000,"metadata":{"description":"The Copilot Studio agent engagement, phase by phase — what must be true in the tenant, the rate card, the caps, the drift register and the recurring service. Seven phases, priced and staffed, two of them before any paper. For SMB customers on Business Premium with zero Copilot seats.","page_type":"engagement","priority":3,"title":"The Copilot Studio Agent Engagement","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.61314064,"vector_rank":5,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"1a9ce9c4c084c2a711f48d477599550123d607cda1aa624903d7b2520632e05f","type":"text","score":0.6279267605379791,"text":"## 7 · Phase 2 · The Licensing Path\n\nNobody in this market is buying E5\\. Assume that as the starting condition rather than the objection, and the licensing conversation becomes short and winnable.\n\nPartners lose it by presenting a feature matrix. The customer does not want to know what E5 includes. They want to know what to buy on Monday, and whether you are about to double their bill. This section is built as decisions, not comparisons.\n\nMS list published Microsoft price or documented entitlement Survey named survey with a stated sample Range community-reported, multiple independent sources Model composed here from cited inputs — a construction, not a citation \n\n### 7.1 · The number that changes the conversation\n\nThe reflexive channel pitch is “you need to upgrade to E5.” A Business Premium customer hears that as _double my Microsoft bill_, and the deal stalls. It has also been wrong since September 2025, when Microsoft started selling the governance stack as add-ons scoped specifically to Business Premium.\n\n| Component                              | Price                    | Notes                                                                                                                                       |\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Microsoft 365 Business Premium (base)  | **$22** /user/mo Range   | Held at this price through the July 2026 adjustments. Reseller-corroborated rather than read off a Microsoft page.                          |\n| **Purview Suite for Business Premium** | **$10** /user/mo MS list | Microsoft's words: _“$10.00 user/month, paid yearly”_, _“Maximum of 300 seats”_, _“Requires a Microsoft 365 Business Premium subscription”_ |\n| **Defender + Purview Suites combined** | **$15** /user/mo Range   | Widely and consistently reported; not read off a Microsoft page in this research                                                            |\n| \\= **the SMB governance stack**        | **\\~$37** /user/mo Model | Business Premium plus both suites                                                                                                           |\n| Microsoft 365 E5, for comparison       | **$60** /user/mo Range   | Rose from $57 on 1 July 2026                                                                                                                |\n\nMicrosoft is funding this motion directly, and it expires\n\n**50% off Purview Suite for Business Premium when purchased with Microsoft 365 Copilot**, available **1 December 2025 through 31 December 2026** MS list. Microsoft's own page frames it around smooth AI rollout. Distribution is running first-year promotions alongside it, several expiring on the same date, and funded deployment services are available on top — see [section 15.5](#section-15).\n\nVerify current terms with your TD SYNNEX rep before quoting. Promotions move.\n\n### 7.2 · The two mis-sales\n\nMis-sale 1\n\n#### E3 is not the compliance step-up customers think it is\n\nMicrosoft's own DLP documentation marks **“restrict Copilot from processing files and emails” as unavailable on Business Premium _and_ unavailable on E3.** Only the E5 family and the Purview Suite line carry it.\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-readiness-engagement.html","timestamp":1787875200000,"metadata":{"title":"The Copilot Readiness Engagement","description":"The Copilot Readiness Engagement — seven phases from the first scan to recurring governance, for SMB customers on Business Premium who are not buying E5. Priced, staffed, and grounded in Microsoft documentation.","page_type":"engagement","priority":3,"chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"keyword_score":11.58566756581803,"keyword_rank":6,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"6134b353d0773b88ca40ea6c1031afdf5928b58875129314543dd203e8a01022","type":"text","score":0.6222258148329917,"text":"## 3 · Phase 2 · The Licensing Decision\n\nDecisions, not a feature matrix. The customer wants to know what to buy on Monday and whether you are about to double their bill.\n\n### 3.1 · The prices\n\n| Component                             | Price                    | Notes                                                                                                                                 |\n| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Microsoft 365 Business Premium (base) | $22 /user/mo Range       | Held at this price through the July 2026 adjustments. Reseller-corroborated.                                                          |\n| Purview Suite for Business Premium    | $10 /user/mo MS list     | Microsoft's words: “$10.00 user/month, paid yearly”, “Maximum of 300 seats”, “Requires a Microsoft 365 Business Premium subscription” |\n| Defender + Purview Suites combined    | $15 /user/mo Range       | Widely and consistently reported; not read off a Microsoft page in this research.                                                     |\n| **\\= the SMB governance stack**       | **\\~$37 /user/mo** Model | Business Premium plus both suites                                                                                                     |\n| Microsoft 365 E5, for comparison      | $60 /user/mo Range       | Rose from $57 on 1 July 2026                                                                                                          |\n\nMicrosoft is funding this motion, and it expires\n\n**50% off Purview Suite for Business Premium when purchased with Microsoft 365 Copilot**, 1 December 2025 through 31 December 2026 MS list. Distribution is running first-year promotions alongside it, several expiring on the same date. **Verify current terms with your TD SYNNEX rep before quoting.**\n\n### 3.2 · The decision table\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-readiness-governance.html","timestamp":1787875200000,"metadata":{"title":"The Copilot Readiness &amp; Governance Engagement","description":"The Copilot readiness and governance engagement, phase by phase — the decisions, gates, checklists, effort bands and prices a partner needs in the room. For SMB customers on Business Premium who are not buying E5.","page_type":"engagement","priority":3,"chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"keyword_score":10.882874002860422,"keyword_rank":7,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"bbb8ca5ede0bad712d6e4a9daa3796848e32348203f1ae80081180a23dd2c04d","type":"text","score":0.6113196578321463,"text":"**Copilot Cowork (March 2026):** The shift from conversational AI to agentic AI. Users can delegate multi-step, long-running tasks — scheduling, research, document generation — that run autonomously in the background. Built into the Copilot license. Currently in Frontier program preview and represents the most significant new adoption driver in the SMB market.\n\n**The 300-seat hard cap** applies to Copilot Business and all Business SKUs. Customers crossing that threshold must migrate to E3 + Microsoft 365 Copilot (Enterprise) — a significant licensing uplift event.\n\n**Purview governance by tier:** Business Premium → basic/manual governance only. E3 → foundational Purview. E5 → full automated governance. This progression is the compliance upsell story at every stage.\n\nStage-by-Stage Field Guide\n\nEach card below covers one rung of the ladder. **The Demo** is the scene you set. **The Pivot** is the line that advances the conversation. **Partner Revenue** is what you write on the whiteboard before you leave.\n\n1\n\nCopilot Chat\n\nFree\n\nNo M365 integration\n\n🎬 The Demo\n\nShow how to use Copilot Chat on the web to summarize a public PDF or write a generic marketing email.\n\n🔄 The Pivot\n\n\"This is great for public data, but it can't see your company's files, emails, or Teams chats. To do real work, we need to connect it to your data.\"\n\n💰 Partner Revenue\n\nZero direct revenue. This is purely a conversation starter — the door into the rest of the ladder.\n\n2\n\nM365 Copilot Business\n\n$21 / user / mo\n\nMax 300 seats\n\n🎬 The Demo — \"The Blank Page Miracle\"\n\nOpen Word, type \"Draft a proposal based on yesterday's Teams meeting and the pricing spreadsheet,\" and watch it generate a full document in seconds.\n\n🔄 The Pivot\n\n\"This is the game-changer. But remember, it can see everything the user has access to. We need to make sure your permissions are locked down first.\"\n\n💰 Partner Revenue\n\nLicensing margin + **$5,000–$10,000** Data Readiness Assessment (SharePoint cleanup, basic permissions audit).\n\n2.5\n\nPersonal Agent Creation\n\nIncluded in Copilot Business\n\nEnablement required\n\n🎬 The Demo — \"The DIY Expert\"\n\nOpen the Copilot app in Teams. Build a live HR Policy Agent in under five minutes, ask it a real question, and share it with someone in the room.\n\n🔄 The Pivot\n\n\"Every one of your licensed users can do exactly what I just did — right now, with no IT ticket. But to get the most out of this, your team needs to know how to build and prompt these correctly.\"\n\n💰 Partner Revenue\n\n**$1,500–$3,000** Enablement Workshops. This is a pipeline stage — you get 1:1 time with department heads to uncover complex use cases that lead to Copilot Studio projects.\n\n3\n\nM365 Business Premium\n\n$22 / user / mo\n\nBasic DLP only\n\n🎬 The Demo\n\nShow the Microsoft Purview dashboard. Show how a document tagged \"Confidential\" cannot be summarized or shared externally by Copilot.\n\n🔄 The Pivot\n\n\"Copilot Business gives you the AI. Business Premium gives you the security to use it safely. You cannot deploy AI without upgrading your security posture.\"\n\n💰 Partner Revenue\n\nLicensing upgrade margin + **$10,000–$15,000** Security Deployment (Intune, Defender, basic Purview setup).\n\n3.5\n\nCopilot Cowork — The Agentic Shift\n\nIncluded in M365 Copilot\n\nFrontier preview\n\n🎬 The Demo — \"Delegate and Forget\"\n\nPrompt: \"Build a meeting packet for the Contoso pitch.\" Watch Cowork autonomously pull emails, draft a briefing doc, build a PowerPoint, and schedule prep time — all running in the background.\n\n🔄 The Pivot\n\n\"Until now, Copilot was an assistant you had to micromanage. Cowork is an employee you can delegate to. But because it acts autonomously across all your data, your data governance must be flawless.\"\n\n💰 Partner Revenue\n\nThe ultimate adoption driver. Accelerates the need for Business Premium/Purview deployments and creates new managed services opportunities around workflow optimization and AgentOps.\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/cpb-model-the-practice.html","timestamp":1787875200000,"metadata":{"description":"The business model behind the practice: the Show-and-Tell sales motion, the Agent Maturity ladder, and the pricing and revenue architecture from first deployment to Managed AgentOps.","page_type":"playbook","priority":3,"title":"Model the Practice","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.59774745,"vector_rank":9,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"1a9ce9c4c084c2a711f48d4775995501abd8c556d46fb0ef5ce1b7aec7bf78c1","type":"text","score":0.6061002826960273,"text":"## 2 · The Short Version — They Are Not Buying E5\n\nEverything after this section is detail. If you read nothing else, read this page.\n\nYour customer is on Business Premium. They are not buying E5\\. _They do not need to._\n\nNobody has to forklift. The licenses you sold are not wrong — the AI conversation added one requirement they were never scoped for, and it is addressable for a few dollars a seat.\n\n### 2.1 · What your customers actually own\n\nAcross the 25–300 seat market the answer is almost always **Microsoft 365 Business Premium**, often mixed with Business Standard on the seats nobody got around to upgrading. That is the normal case, not the failure case, and this guide is written for it.\n\nIt matters because Business Premium was sold, correctly, as a complete small-business security stack — Entra ID P1, Defender for Business, Intune, Defender for Office. It is a good SKU and it did the job it was bought for. Copilot did not make it inadequate. Copilot added _one_ requirement that nobody was scoping for in 2023.\n\n### 2.2 · Split the problem in two, because the licensing does\n\nHalf one — data protection\n\n#### Fully reachable on Business Premium\n\nSensitivity labels, DLP, retention, insider risk, audit, and the controls that keep labeled content out of Copilot — all of it, through the **Purview Suite for Business Premium** add-on.\n\n* **$10** per user per month MS list, 300-seat cap\n* **$15** combined with the Defender Suite Range\n* Total stack **\\~$37** against E5 at **$60** Model\n* **Half price alongside Copilot until 31 December 2026** MS list\n\nThere is no gap here. Do not let anyone tell your customer they need E5 for this.\n\nHalf two — permissions\n\n#### The one thing Business Premium cannot reach\n\nSharePoint Advanced Management — the oversharing toolkit. Data Access Governance reports, Everyone Except External Users insights, Restricted Content Discovery, site access reviews.\n\nMicrosoft requires an **Office 365 or Microsoft 365 E-SKU base** for it. Business Premium and Business Standard are not on the list, and buying Copilot does not change that. Full detail and the verbatim requirement in [section 9](#section-9).\n\n**That is the entire gap.** One toolkit, one requirement. Everything else in this guide is reachable on the licenses your customer already owns.\n\n### 2.3 · Three supported ways to cover it. None of them is E5.\n\nEvery option below is documented, buyable today, and produces a defensible engagement. They differ in cost, in effort, and in what they leave you unable to do — so the risks are stated with each one rather than buried.\n\n1\n\nDeliver it natively on Business Premium\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-readiness-engagement.html","timestamp":1787875200000,"metadata":{"title":"The Copilot Readiness Engagement","description":"The Copilot Readiness Engagement — seven phases from the first scan to recurring governance, for SMB customers on Business Premium who are not buying E5. Priced, staffed, and grounded in Microsoft documentation.","page_type":"engagement","priority":3,"chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"keyword_score":9.944386411693309,"keyword_rank":10,"fusion_method":"rrf"}},{"id":"1a9ce9c4c084c2a711f48d47759955017dc6d93b9574e72212b2e63a5d99b31d","type":"text","score":0.6010279322116302,"text":"### 7.5 · The decision table\n\n| If the customer needs…                                                            | Buy                                           | Because                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Labels, DLP, insider risk, Copilot interaction controls, audit retention          | **Business Premium + Purview Suite**          | $10/user/mo, 300-seat cap, half price alongside Copilot through 31 Dec 2026                                                                                                                                            |\n| Shadow AI discovery and full CASB                                                 | **\\+ Defender Suite** (combined $15)          | Business Premium has no CASB. Verify that the suite carries full Defender for Cloud Apps before promising discovery.                                                                                                   |\n| **DAG reports, EEEU insights, Restricted Content Discovery, site access reviews** | **An E-SKU base** — or a third-party platform | The wall in [section 9](#section-9). No Business Premium add-on reaches SharePoint Advanced Management.                                                                                                                |\n| Auto-labeling of files and emails                                                 | E5 family Verify                              | Microsoft states the symptom for “E3 or Business Premium” explicitly. Whether the Purview Suite lifts it is unconfirmed — test before scoping.                                                                         |\n| Copilot itself, under 300 seats                                                   | Copilot Business SKUs                         | **Sequencing trap:** you cannot upgrade to an Enterprise plan from Business Standard or Premium with Copilot Business until commitment end. A growing customer can be stuck for up to a year. Decide before attaching. |\n| Security Copilot                                                                  | Do not sell it here                           | Provisioned capacity is priced per hour and the included-capacity model is E5/E7 only. Not an SMB motion.                                                                                                              |\n\n### 7.6 · Two things that belong in the engagement letter\n\nThese are delegated-administration constraints, and they will surprise you mid-engagement rather than at scoping.\n\n","item":{"key":"https://copilotplaybook.com/copilot-readiness-engagement.html","timestamp":1787875200000,"metadata":{"description":"The Copilot Readiness Engagement — seven phases from the first scan to recurring governance, for SMB customers on Business Premium who are not buying E5. Priced, staffed, and grounded in Microsoft documentation.","page_type":"engagement","priority":3,"title":"The Copilot Readiness Engagement","chunk_modality":"text"}},"scoring_details":{"vector_score":0.59689623,"vector_rank":11,"fusion_method":"rrf"}}]

